  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nDeep moisture has shifted substantially as expected and has actually \nleft us with little to no shower activity this morning. Any sea \nbreeze activity will be very limited today as satellite estimated \nPWAT values are at or below normal for most of the area. Moisture \nremains low through Saturday and only a notch better for Sunday - \nThis is a change from yesterday when guidance had suggested more of \na moisture push coming into the region. Have only silent 10 pops \nwith the sea breeze for Sunday. By Monday, high pressure is fully in \ncontrol, thus PoPs remain quite low. Moisture does increase though, \nso an isolated sea breeze shower/storm is possible. The next chance \nfor more than a low chance will be Thursday as a wave of moisture \ncomes in and a short wave rotates around at the same time. At this \ntime a moderate chance of showers/storms at that time. \n\nHigh temperatures remain above normal at least through the middle of \nnext week with heat index as ridging builds. Heat index values above \n110 will be likely again especially east on Saturday, thus another \nheat advisory is likely. We'll remain borderline on heat advisory \nconditions for Sunday and Monday with lower chances into next week. \n\nBreezy conditions are expected through the weekend and into early \nnext week in the afternoons and evenings. This, combined with low RH \nvalues over the Brush country may result in elevated fire weather \nconditions over that region.",
